docs/decisions.md:558:| OD1 | <a id="od1"></a>References: the Austral model. A reference is formed by borrowing a variable or projecting through a reference; `&s.f` is sugar; a temporary is never a root; loans are keyed by the root variable; a raw-pointer dereference needs a trusted validity premise | D2 and §4.5; implemented in landing train 6 (REF-5, REF-11) | exit 2i (G12), so every deliverable; bug 182's repair (exit 0b); V2 (row 6) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: the Austral model: a reference is formed by borrowing a variable or projecting through a reference; `&s.f` is sugar; a temporary is never a root; references are second-class, never stored or returned; a raw-pointer dereference needs a trusted validity premise; loans are keyed by the root variable (confirmed by OD15 (a), decided 2026-10-29) |
docs/decisions.md:594:| OD37 | <a id="od37"></a>The names of the pointer difference and ordering functions (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD26). Options: `ptr::diff(p, q) -> Int` and `ptr::cmp(p, q) -> Ordering` beside `ptr::offset`; or no ordering at all, only `ptr::addr(p)` (via `ptr::expose`) compared as integers. Recommendation: `ptr::diff` and `ptr::cmp`, both trusted, each with its provenance premise (same allocation) stated as an obligation; Austral has no named pointer-difference or ordering functions, and its `Pointer` and `Address` types are compared with the ordering operators Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): one trusted `ptr::diff` (same-allocation premise), ordering read from its sign, no `ptr::cmp`; specify the representability of its result, one-past pointers included (Q8 is marked agreed in the picks; ROADMAP records no owner answer for it) | trusted code compares raw pointers with `==` and `<` today (bug 322) | bug 322's repair (TYPE-6, TRUST-1) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One trusted `ptr::diff`, ordering from its sign, no `ptr::cmp` |
docs/decisions.md:669:| OD112 | <a id="od112"></a>Mutation through a shared reference (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a value reached through a shared reference `&T` may be changed, by a write through a raw pointer derived from the reference or by a cell type. Bears on REF-10, OWN-10, TRUST-2, TRUST-5. The current compiler: safe code changes a value only through a `mut` binding or a `&mut` reference, and there is no cell type; a trusted function may convert `r: &Int` to `*mut Int` (`r as *const Int as *mut Int`) and store through it: the binary does the store, and `--interp` stops ("deref-assign through immutable ref"). The archived [MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md) §12 said there is no `Cell`, `RefCell` or `UnsafeCell` equivalent and that mutation flows from `let mut` through `&mut`; the [overview](../legacy/docs/spec/overview.md) lists no interior mutability in safe code, with a later `UnsafeCell<T>` gated by `Unsafe`. Options: (a) never: a write through a pointer derived from a `&T` is undefined behaviour, a premise trusted code keeps (TRUST-2); (b) as (a), with a cell type gated by `Unsafe` as the one exception; (c) a trusted write through such a pointer is allowed. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | safe code cannot; a trusted write through `r as *const T as *mut T` runs in the binary and stops `--interp` | REF-10, OWN-10, TRUST-2, TRUST-5 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Never; UB premise trusted code keeps |
docs/decisions.md:670:| OD113 | <a id="od113"></a>The ownership exceptions of trusted code (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether trusted code keeps its two exceptions to the ownership rules, destroying a value through a reference and releasing with `vec_free` a vector whose elements are not Copy. Bears on TRUST-5, TRUST-12, OWN-5, OWN-8. The current compiler: both are accepted in trusted code only (elsewhere E0290 under OWN-8 and E0287 under OWN-5): std's collections destroy an element through `(p as &T)` in drop glue after its owner was consumed, and a trusted function may free a buffer it has emptied. The archived [MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md) §11 said that `trusted` does not relax linearity, and TRUST-5's rationale says the same. Options: (a) keep both, each a named trusted operation with its premise (the owner was consumed; the vector is empty); (b) remove both, drop glue moving the element out of raw memory instead (TRUST-7); (c) keep only destruction through a reference; (d) keep only `vec_free` of an emptied vector. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | both accepted in trusted code only (E0290, E0287 elsewhere) | TRUST-5, TRUST-12, OWN-5, OWN-8 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove both |
docs/decisions.md:684:| OD127 | <a id="od127"></a>UTF-8 in every `String` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether every `String` value that safe code can reach holds valid UTF-8, as a language guarantee. Bears on TYPE-3, SYN-5, TRUST-2. The current compiler: literals are UTF-8 (SYN-5), but nothing keeps a `String` valid: `push_char` appends a byte unchecked, `Bytes::to_string` transfers bytes without validation, and std's `read_to_string` does not validate. [STRING_TEXT_CONTRACT.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/STRING_TEXT_CONTRACT.md) §1 states the invariant as a target, and [UNICODE_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/UNICODE_POLICY.md) has a validating `Bytes::to_string` returning `Option`. Options: (a) a language guarantee, every safe constructor validating and an unchecked one being a trusted operation with that premise; (b) a std convention only, `String` being bytes to the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-127 | see OPEN-127 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Language guarantee |
docs/decisions.md:1063:- **OPEN-1** (OD1, references): decided 2026-10-29. Now REF-5 (formation, `&s.f`, no temporary root), REF-1 and REF-3 (second-class), TRUST-3 (the raw-pointer validity premise, pending) and REF-8 (loans keyed by the root variable, as OD15 (a) confirmed).
docs/archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md:256:| a pointer obtained earlier for b, after b is released | keeps `prov = b`; any access violates the liveness premise |
docs/archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md:258:Accessing memory through a pointer requires every validity premise for the bytes read or written: provenance, in bounds, alive, aligned, initialized for a read, and write access for a write. A violated premise is a failed proof obligation of the `Unsafe` code. It is never defined behaviour, and the safe theorem assumes these premises (rule 5, `decisions.md:372`). A zero-byte access needs only alignment and a non-null address.
